WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.887
Recall at k is one of the easiest retrieval metrics to understand.

00:00:04.887 --> 00:00:12.397
For each evaluation question, we retrieve the top k chunks and check whether the expected source document appears

00:00:12.397 --> 00:00:19.012
anywhere in that set. If the correct document is present, that question is a hit.

00:00:19.012 --> 00:00:23.266
Recall at k is the proportion of questions that produce a hit.

00:00:23.914 --> 00:00:29.112
Recall at one is stricter because the correct document must be ranked first.

00:00:29.112 --> 00:00:33.151
Recall at three gives the system a little more room.

00:00:33.151 --> 00:00:38.596
This metric does not tell us everything, but it answers an important question:

00:00:38.596 --> 00:00:42.071
did retrieval provide the evidence we needed?

00:00:42.071 --> 00:00:49.842
If recall is poor, changing the language model is usually not the first fix.

00:00:49.842 --> 00:00:55.262
Investigate chunking, metadata, embeddings, filters, and retrieval methods first.

00:00:55.262 --> 00:00:59.144
This is how evaluation helps us target the correct layer.
